//! The UI module: immediate-mode widgets over `Input`'s event queue and `Simp`'s renderer.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.5's immediate-mode UI item, which ADR-0165's event loop unblocked — and the
//! module that proves the graphics stack **composes**, since it is the first thing here that needs a window,
//! an event queue and a renderer at once.
//!
//! # Migrated onto `Simp` twice (ADR-0182 §5, then this session)
//!
//! This module first drew through `Window.set_color`, `fill`, `outline` and `rect`. Group D deleted all
//! four — `Simp` owned the renderer from that point on, and leaving a second way to draw would have been
//! the opposite of a clean cutover. That migration made `draw_button` take a `*Simp.Renderer` and draw
//! through `SDL_RenderGeometry`.
//!
//! **This session's rewrite removes the renderer handle.** `Simp` matches Jai's real signatures now —
//! `set_render_target`, `immediate_begin`, `immediate_quad` and the rest take no state argument, because
//! ADR-0186 gave the language file-scope globals and `Simp` keeps its GL context and vertex batch in one
//! (ADR-0187). So `draw_button` drops its `simp` parameter along with the handle it pointed at, and every
//! call it makes returns nothing rather than `bool #must`: `Simp`'s calls no longer report failure, because
//! the GL calls underneath them are `void` in the same way `Window`'s wrappers never were `bool`.
//!
//! **The interaction is untouched.** `button`, `feed`, the hot/active state machine and the id rules are
//! exactly what they were, and the integration test's assertions are unchanged: the same button, clicked the
//! same way, produces the same exit code while every pixel now goes through OpenGL with real GLSL 1.20
//! shaders instead of `SDL_RenderGeometry`. An unchanged assertion over a replaced backend is the only check
//! that a migration preserved behaviour rather than merely compiling.
//!
//! **The outline is four thin quads**, because a batch of triangles has no line primitive — true of both
//! `SDL_RenderGeometry` and the GL triangles that replaced it. That is a real cost of the batch-of-quads
//! shape and it is written down rather than glossed: one outline became four quads. What it buys is that
//! the body and the outline are now *different colours in one batch* — a renderer-global colour would force
//! one draw call per colour, and a per-vertex colour does not.
//!
//! # The coordinate convention, and why `draw_button` does not flip anything
//!
//! `Simp`'s default is `RIGHT_HANDED`: origin bottom-left, y upwards. This module's own coordinates stay
//! **y-down, origin top-left** — `feed` copies `event.mouse_x`/`mouse_y` straight from `Input.Event`, which
//! reports window pixels the way every mouse event on every platform does, and `inside`, `button`, `is_hot`
//! and `is_active` all compare against that same convention without ever touching `Simp`. Converting inside
//! `draw_button` would fix the picture only: the widget would be drawn where the mouse thinks it is not,
//! because nothing here has, or should have, the render target's height to convert with — `draw_button`
//! takes a rectangle and an id, not a window.
//!
//! **So the contract is the caller's, not this module's**: whatever draws through this UI must call
//! `Simp.set_render_target(window, Simp.LEFT_HANDED)`, which keeps origin top-left, y downwards — SDL's
//! convention and the one every coordinate in this file already assumes. That keeps `is_hot`/`inside`
//! agreeing with where the button is actually drawn, which matters more than which way up the window is:
//! a UI whose hit test and drawing disagree is a UI that is broken, where one that is merely upside down
//! (the rejected alternative) is not.
//!
//! # Immediate mode, and what that buys
//!
//! There is no widget tree and no retained object per button. A caller re-declares every widget every frame:
//!
//! ```text
//! begin_frame(*ui);
//! Input.update_window_events(*events, 64);
//! for_each_event(*ui, *events);
//!
//! if button(*ui, 1, 10, 10, 80, 24) { save(); }
//! if button(*ui, 2, 10, 44, 80, 24) { quit(); }
//! ```
//!
//! The state that must persist between frames is exactly three things — where the mouse is, which widget the
//! cursor is over, and which widget is being pressed — so `UI` is small and a caller never allocates. That is
//! the property worth having in a language whose allocator is a `context` field (ADR-0061): a UI that needs no
//! allocator cannot get one wrong.
//!
//! **Rejected: a retained widget tree.** It reads better for a form with forty fields and it needs an
//! allocator, a lifetime discipline for widget handles, and a diffing pass. Immediate mode is what a language
//! at this stage can carry honestly.
//!
//! # A widget's identity is a number the caller chooses
//!
//! `button(ui, id, …)` takes an `id` the caller must keep unique and stable across frames. That is how a
//! stateless call gets stateful behaviour: `active` holds the id of the widget being pressed, so the press
//! survives into the next frame even though the *call* does not.
//!
//! **Two widgets sharing an id is a bug this module cannot detect**, and saying so is better than pretending
//! otherwise: they would highlight and fire together. A caller with a loop should use the loop index, offset by
//! a per-group base.
//!
//! **`0` is reserved** for "nothing", so a widget must not use it. `id_is_valid` is here to say so, and
//! `button` refuses a zero id rather than silently behaving as though nothing were pressed.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No text.** A label needs a font, and a font needs either `SDL_ttf` (a second library) or a bitmap glyph
//! table this module would carry as data. Both are real; neither is a detail, and a button that draws its own
//! frame is useful without one.
//!
//! **No keyboard focus.** Tab-order needs an ordered widget list, which immediate mode deliberately does not
//! keep — the standard answer is a "focus id" plus a per-frame candidate scan, and it wants the text field
//! that does not exist yet to be worth having.
//!
//! **No layout.** Every widget takes explicit pixels. A row/column stack is a small addition and it is a
//! *policy* decision (does a stack own spacing? does it clip?) rather than a mechanism, so it waits for a
//! caller with an opinion.

Input :: #import "Input";
Simp :: #import "Simp";

// Imported **aliased** (ADR-0179 §1), so every name this module borrows says where it comes from:
// `Input.Event`, `Input.MOUSE_DOWN`, `Simp.immediate_quad`. A bare `#import` merges a module's names into
// this file flat, which is why this file used to have to pick names that did not collide with `Window`'s
// eleven unprefixed ones — the id sentinel below is called `NONE` for exactly that reason, and it no longer
// has to be. Two aliased imports of modules that both export `Event`-adjacent names is also a program that
// could not have been written before ADR-0179.

/// The reserved widget id meaning "no widget".
NONE :: 0;

/// How thick `draw_button`'s outline is, in pixels.
///
/// Named because a hairline is invisible on a high-DPI target and a caller who wants a different weight
/// should be able to find the number — and because an outline made of quads has a thickness at all, which a
/// line primitive did not (ADR-0182 §5).
OUTLINE_THICKNESS :: 1.0;

/// Per-frame and persistent UI state.
///
/// Small on purpose: a caller declares one, keeps it between frames, and never allocates. Every field is
/// written by `feed` or by a widget call, and none by the caller.
UI :: struct {
    /// Where the cursor is, in window pixels.
    mouse_x: s32;
    /// Where the cursor is, in window pixels.
    mouse_y: s32;
    /// Whether the left button is currently held.
    ///
    /// Persistent across frames, because a press that begins in one frame and ends in another is the normal
    /// case at sixty frames a second.
    mouse_down: bool;
    /// Whether the left button went down **during this frame**. Cleared by `begin_frame`.
    pressed_this_frame: bool;
    /// Whether the left button came up **during this frame**. Cleared by `begin_frame`.
    released_this_frame: bool;
    /// The id of the widget under the cursor, or `NONE`.
    ///
    /// Recomputed every frame, by each widget's own hit test — which is why it is cleared in `begin_frame`
    /// rather than remembered: a widget that stopped being declared must stop being hot.
    hot: s64;
    /// The id of the widget being pressed, or `NONE`.
    ///
    /// **Not** cleared by `begin_frame`: it is the one piece of genuinely multi-frame state, and clearing it
    /// is what makes a naive immediate-mode button fire on press instead of on release.
    active: s64;
}

/// Whether `id` can name a widget.
///
/// `NONE` cannot, because `active == NONE` means "nothing is pressed" and a widget with that id would be
/// indistinguishable from the absence of one.
id_is_valid :: (id: s64) -> bool {
    return id != NONE;
}

/// Starts a frame: clears the per-frame edges and forgets which widget was hot.
///
/// `active` deliberately survives, since a press spans frames. `hot` does not, because a widget the caller
/// stopped declaring must stop being hot — and recomputing it is what makes that automatic rather than a thing
/// the caller has to remember.
///
/// Must be called before any `feed` for the frame, and before any widget.
begin_frame :: (ui: *UI) {
    ui.pressed_this_frame = false;
    ui.released_this_frame = false;
    ui.hot = NONE;
}

/// Folds one event into `ui`.
///
/// Ignores every event kind it does not use, so a caller can pass the whole queue through it without
/// filtering — which is what makes the loop in the module docs a loop rather than a switch.
///
/// Only the left button is tracked (`mouse_button == 1`). A right-click is a different interaction with
/// different conventions on every platform, and guessing at it would be worse than not having it.
feed :: (ui: *UI, event: *Input.Event) {
    kind := cast(s64, event.kind);
    if kind == Input.MOUSE_MOTION {
        ui.mouse_x = event.mouse_x;
        ui.mouse_y = event.mouse_y;
        return;
    }
    if kind == Input.MOUSE_DOWN {
        if cast(s64, event.mouse_button) == 1 {
            // The position comes from the event, not from the last motion: a click can arrive without a
            // preceding motion event — a tap on a trackpad, or a synthetic event — and using a stale position
            // would hit-test the wrong widget.
            ui.mouse_x = event.mouse_x;
            ui.mouse_y = event.mouse_y;
            ui.mouse_down = true;
            ui.pressed_this_frame = true;
        }
        return;
    }
    if kind == Input.MOUSE_UP {
        if cast(s64, event.mouse_button) == 1 {
            ui.mouse_x = event.mouse_x;
            ui.mouse_y = event.mouse_y;
            ui.mouse_down = false;
            ui.released_this_frame = true;
        }
    }
}

/// Whether `(ui.mouse_x, ui.mouse_y)` is inside the rectangle.
///
/// Half-open on the far edges — `x <= px < x + w` — so two rectangles that share an edge do not both claim the
/// pixel on it. That is the same convention every drawing API here uses for a fill, and disagreeing with it
/// would make a two-button row have a one-pixel column that highlights both.
inside :: (ui: *UI, x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64) -> bool {
    px := cast(s64, ui.mouse_x);
    py := cast(s64, ui.mouse_y);
    return px >= x && px < x + w && py >= y && py < y + h;
}

/// Declares a button, and reports whether it was clicked this frame.
///
/// **A click is a release inside, after a press inside** — never a press. That is what a button does everywhere,
/// and it is the thing a naive immediate-mode implementation gets wrong: returning `true` on press means a
/// caller who drags off the button and releases elsewhere still fires it, which is exactly the escape hatch a
/// user expects to work.
///
/// So the sequence is: press inside sets `active` to this id; release with `active == id` **and the cursor still
/// inside** is a click; release anywhere else clears `active` and fires nothing. A press outside sets `active`
/// to nothing, so a drag *onto* a button from elsewhere cannot arm it either.
///
/// A zero id fires nothing and is not made hot: see `id_is_valid`.
button :: (ui: *UI, id: s64, x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64) -> bool {
    if !id_is_valid(id) {
        return false;
    }

    over := inside(ui, x, y, w, h);
    if over {
        ui.hot = id;
    }

    if ui.pressed_this_frame && over {
        ui.active = id;
    }

    if ui.released_this_frame {
        if ui.active == id {
            ui.active = NONE;
            // The release must be over the widget too. Checked here rather than at press time, because that is
            // where the difference between a click and an abandoned press lives.
            return over;
        }
    }

    return false;
}

/// Whether `id` is the widget under the cursor.
///
/// For drawing: a caller picks a hover colour from this. Valid only after the widget's own call, since that
/// call is what sets `hot`.
is_hot :: (ui: *UI, id: s64) -> bool {
    // The zero id is refused rather than compared, and this was a real bug the tests caught: `hot` *is* `NONE`
    // whenever nothing is hot, so a bare `ui.hot == id` answered **true** for `is_hot(ui, NONE)` on every frame.
    // A sentinel that means "nothing" must not be askable about, or every "is this widget hot" question has a
    // widget for which the answer is yes and the widget does not exist.
    if !id_is_valid(id) {
        return false;
    }
    return ui.hot == id;
}

/// Whether `id` is the widget being pressed.
///
/// For drawing a pressed state. True while the button is held, including after the cursor has left — which is
/// right: the widget is still armed, and showing it un-pressed would tell the user the release will do nothing
/// when moving back would still fire it.
is_active :: (ui: *UI, id: s64) -> bool {
    // Refused for the same reason as `is_hot`: `active == NONE` means "nothing is pressed".
    if !id_is_valid(id) {
        return false;
    }
    return ui.active == id;
}

/// Draws a button's frame in one of three shades, by its state.
///
/// Separate from `button` on purpose. Immediate-mode libraries usually draw inside the widget call, which
/// couples hit-testing to a renderer and makes the logic untestable without a window. Splitting them lets the
/// state machine be exercised with no display at all — which is how this module's tests run — and lets a caller
/// draw a button any way they like while keeping the interaction.
///
/// **One batch, five quads, two colours.** The body is a filled quad and the outline is four thin ones,
/// because a batch of triangles has no line primitive. All five go in one `immediate_begin`/
/// `immediate_flush` pair, which a renderer-global colour could not do: a per-vertex colour means one
/// flush can carry quads of different colours where the old `SDL_SetRenderDrawColor` path forced a draw
/// call each.
///
/// Coordinates are `x`/`y`/`w`/`h` as the caller and `button` already use them — top-left origin, y
/// downwards — and are drawn as written, with no flip. See the module doc's coordinate-convention
/// section: the caller's `Simp.set_render_target` call, not this procedure, is what keeps that agreeing
/// with the pixels `Simp` actually draws.
///
/// **Returns whether the button fired**, which is what a caller wants and what Jai's `GetRect.button`
/// does: it draws and reports the click in one call.
///
/// It used to return `bool #must`, and that bool was the *flush's* success — `return
/// Simp.immediate_flush(simp);` — which is not a thing a caller can act on and is no longer reported at
/// all, since the new `Simp` calls return nothing. Dropping the return entirely was the first migration,
/// and it was wrong: it left a caller unable to know whether the button was pressed without a second
/// `button` call, and it turned an integration test's `if draw_button(…)` into an unconditional
/// assertion. So the value returned changed *meaning* rather than disappearing.
///
/// The interaction is still `button`'s, called once here. Calling both `button` and `draw_button` for one
/// widget in one frame is a mistake — the state machine would see two presses — so a caller uses one or
/// the other, and this one is the shape Jai has.
draw_button :: (ui: *UI, id: s64, x: s64, y: s64, w: s64, h: s64) -> bool {
    // Read the interaction **before** drawing, so the shades below reflect the state this frame ends in
    // rather than the one it started in. A button clicked this frame draws as active, which is what a
    // user sees in any immediate-mode UI; drawing first would show the previous frame's state and feel
    // like a one-frame lag.
    fired := button(ui, id, x, y, w, h);
    shade := 70;
    if is_active(ui, id) {
        shade = 130;
    } else if is_hot(ui, id) {
        shade = 100;
    }

    // The shade is 0-255 in this module's own terms and `Simp`'s colours are 0..1, so the conversion happens
    // here, once. Kept as an integer up to this point because the three states are integers a reader can
    // compare, which `0.27`, `0.39` and `0.51` would not be.
    level := cast(float32, shade) / 255.0;
    body := Simp.vector4(level, level, level, 1.0);
    edge := Simp.vector4(0.78, 0.78, 0.78, 1.0);

    Simp.set_shader_for_color(false);
    Simp.immediate_begin();

    left := cast(float32, x);
    top := cast(float32, y);
    right := cast(float32, x + w);
    bottom := cast(float32, y + h);

    Simp.immediate_quad(left, top, right, bottom, body);

    // The outline, inset nowhere: four quads one pixel thick along the edges, drawn *after* the body so they
    // win where they overlap. `OUTLINE_THICKNESS` rather than a bare `1.0` because a hairline is invisible on
    // a high-DPI target, and a caller who wants a different weight should be able to find the number.
    // `cast` because `OUTLINE_THICKNESS` is a `float64`: a Jairs constant has no width annotation — probed,
    // `OUTLINE_THICKNESS : float32 : 1.0` does not parse — which is the typed-constant gap ADR-0165 §5 already
    // owed for `QUIT : u32 : 256`. The narrowing is exact for a small integral value, so this cannot be wrong.
    t := cast(float32, OUTLINE_THICKNESS);
    Simp.immediate_quad(left, top, right, top + t, edge);
    Simp.immediate_quad(left, bottom - t, right, bottom, edge);
    Simp.immediate_quad(left, top, left + t, bottom, edge);
    Simp.immediate_quad(right - t, top, right, bottom, edge);

    Simp.immediate_flush();
    return fired;
}
